2026-11-16 13:28:11 | RESPONSE | sactor.llm:160 | Crown reports `img_pixels` as `Arr` of `Arr` with `Mut` at both levels, so it is a pointer to an array of row pointers, and each row pointer points to an array of pixels. That is a 2D pixel grid. Ownership is Unknown, but the bitmap image evidently owns its pixel data, so I treat it as owning.

The idiomatic form is `Vec<Vec<_bmp_pixel>>`, with one inner `Vec` per row.

The C struct has no explicit length fields. The dimensions live in `img_header.biHeight` and `biWidth`, so the spec uses those as the lengths. `biHeight` can be negative in BMP files, meaning a top-down layout. That is why the spec notes the absolute value.

`_bmp_header` derives `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug` and `Default`. `_bmp_pixel` derives `Copy`, `Clone` and `Debug`. So the idiomatic struct can derive `Clone` and `Debug`. It cannot be `Copy` because it contains `Vec`.

In the spec, the idiomatic `img_pixels` is a nested `Vec`. The outer length comes from `img_header.biHeight`, and the inner length from `img_header.biWidth`. The `ptr` shape allows only one `len_from`, so I describe the outer slice with `biHeight` and add a note about the rows.

----STRUCT----
```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct _bmp_img {
    pub img_header: _bmp_header,
    pub img_pixels: Vec<Vec<_bmp_pixel>>,
}
```
